{"id":"lane-oge-address","parent":"odu-green-exit-nonzero","ord":"a7","title":"address findings","done":"2026-08-31T17:47:27-04:00","started":"2026-08-31T17:41:50-04:00","worked":764,"after":["lane-oge-review"],"created":"2026-08-31T17:17:21-04:00","changed":"2026-08-31T17:47:27-04:00"}
{"id":"odu-wait-settle-short","parent":"oduc-agent","ord":"a1","title":"odu: `wait --settle` returns before the run settles","done":"2026-09-01T10:54:35-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T08:22:05-04:00","worked":9150,"desc":"Observed while dogfooding odu on its own PR: the CLI's `odu wait --settle` came back after about fifteen seconds saying `{\\\"settled\\\": false, \\\"passed\\\": false}` with exit code 1 — no reason given, no fail-fast, no timeout, no cancellation — while the run was demonstrably still going, lanes still leased and phases still running.\n\nThe MCP path did the right thing against that same live run: `wait_for_settle` blocked properly and returned the true verdict when it settled. So the two paths disagree, and the CLI is the one that lies.\n\nWhy it matters: a caller that trusts the CLI's answer reads a healthy run as an unsettled failure — which is the same class of false signal as the exit-code bug this run was pinning. Worth looking for a stray short-circuit in the CLI path that the MCP verb does not share.\n\n**QUEUED, AND IT SHIPS WITH THE RETRY DESCRIPTION (the human, 2026-08-31 ~20:45):** this and `odu-mcp-rerun-doc` are ONE PR, not two lanes — one story, told twice: the CLI lies about settling, and the retry verb never says what it does. Auto-merge. First in the overnight queue, dispatched when the appliance fold merges (the human ruled strictly one lane at a time). Both platforms green is the bar for odu, ruled the same evening.","see":["odu-mcp-rerun-doc"],"created":"2026-08-31T18:02:07-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:54:35-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/odu-wait-settle.md","dispatched":"2026-09-01T08:18:00-04:00","from":"the CI watcher on odu#95's run, 2026-08-31 — reported as a harness note, unrelated to that PR","kolu-terminal":"f1752950","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/odu/commit/a2f2050e","merged":"2026-09-01","odu-worktree":".worktrees/odu-wait-settle","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/odu/pull/96","repo":"repo-odu","tier":"design-heavy","timings":"implement 45m55s (opus) · refactor 18m41s · reviews 2 rounds each 20m45s/34m03s parallel (grok 1 MUST 5 SHOULD, pi 0 MUST 4 SHOULD) · address 2 rounds 19m58s · merge-master skipped · ci 28m04s incl a clean darwin re-run (14/14 both platforms) · close <1m — TOTAL 2h36m (08:18→10:54)"}}
{"id":"lane-ows-implement","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a0","title":"implement + open PR","done":"2026-09-01T09:08:00-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T08:22:05-04:00","worked":2755,"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T09:08:00-04:00"}
{"id":"lane-ows-refactor","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a1","title":"refactor passes","done":"2026-09-01T09:26:41-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T09:08:00-04:00","worked":1121,"after":["lane-ows-implement"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T09:26:41-04:00"}
{"id":"lane-ows-review-a","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a2","title":"review: grok","done":"2026-09-01T10:17:32-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T10:10:30-04:00","worked":1245,"desc":"Two rounds. Round 1 at 421bde29: MUST-fix, 3 SHOULD, 2 NIT — it refuted the author's own diagnosis from the pre-change code, and found that the fix repaired tagged deaths while routing a second transport-loss class into the arm that still lied. Round 2 at 891a8d78: APPROVE, MUST closed, 2 SHOULD.\n\n**The re-verdict is a review OF THE NEW PINS, one by one, and it graded them differently — which is the point.**\n- `server.test.ts:666` HOLDS, and it explained why the staging is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: interrupt is not `isDeadTransportError`, so on the pre-fix clean-end arm it is exactly the nothing-shape, while a TAGGED pass 2 would have been green on that buggy code because the catch already re-subscribed. So the test fails on the pre-fix code FOR THE MUST, and a pass afterwards cannot be the catch arm pretending.\n- `server.test.ts:610` HOLDS for the orDie question — `dyingOnce` injects after the projection, so `pass === 2` is the right witness that the defect survived `Stream.orDie` as a recognisable one.\n- **`server.test.ts:720` — \"the pause is real; the story is not.\"** Every `get()` tagged-fails with zero frames, so `lastLive` is never set, and a bug that answered tagged death as end-of-stream would throw `NoLiveRunError` rather than return the nothing-shape. **The test cannot produce the verdict it names.** What it does pin is the attempt pacing, which is worth keeping — but the captured-link-after-a-live-frame sequence it claims to cover is still unpinned.\n\nThat is the FOURTH test this week found to prove less than it appeared to, and the first where the assertion is real while the STORY around it is wrong.\n\nIts other SHOULD: two comments still say both faces use one reader, which is the leftover of the claim the equality test used to fake — the function's own jsdoc already carries the true sentence.","after":["lane-ows-refactor"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:17:32-04:00","custom":{"kolu-terminal":"53c6512b","musts":"1","reviewer":"agent-grok","shoulds":"5"}}
{"id":"lane-ows-review-b","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a3","title":"review: pi","done":"2026-09-01T10:19:42-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T10:10:30-04:00","worked":2043,"desc":"Two rounds. Round 1 at 421bde29: approve, 0 MUST, 2 SHOULD, 2 NIT — where grok returned MUST-fix on the same head. Round 2 at 891a8d78: approve, 1 NIT, and an explicit concession.\n\n**It conceded its half of the disagreement precisely**, which is what makes the disagreement worth having: \"grok's claim and mine were about different things, and his was the one that mattered — `endOnInterrupt` MANUFACTURES a clean end out of an interrupt-only Cause, and a zero-frame manufactured end walked straight into `streamEndedVerdict()` with `lastLive` already set. I had judged that case fail-closed-and-acceptable on the assumption the ledger answers there; with no finalized record it answers NOTHING, and that is a lie of the same species. My `Cause.empty` fact was true and beside the point.\" It also accepted that the staged repro is one real scenario rather than two stitched together, since after a keep-alive death the next pass is definitionally that dial.\n\n**It then attacked the new MUST pin rather than accepting the fix it had been wrong about.** Red on the pre-fix commit through exactly the deleted path, against a null `resolveRunContext` so the ledger cannot mask it; green on head only via the real third pass's done frame, with `pass === 3` ruling out an accidental early answer. It checked the death literal is a genuine one and that the attempt bounds genuinely discriminate a hot loop (thousands) from a paced one (3).\n\n**And it named the completeness argument the fix rests on:** the four-quadrant matrix over {delivered, zero} × {clean, tagged} is complete, the abort signal is consulted before the fall-through so an aborted controller cannot spin the zero-frame path, and a future reader that violated the speaks-at-least-once contract degrades to a paced `timed_out: true` — loud rather than a lie.","after":["lane-ows-refactor"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:19:42-04:00","custom":{"kolu-terminal":"268aba35","musts":"0","reviewer":"agent-pi","shoulds":"2"}}
{"id":"lane-ows-merge-master","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a4","title":"merge latest master into the PR","cancelled":"2026-09-01T10:25:44-04:00","desc":"SKIPPED: odu's master is 374c16c and this branch is 0 commits behind it, so the merge would be empty. The step's purpose — the watcher tests the code as it will actually land — is already satisfied. Re-opened and run if master moves before the merge.","after":["lane-ows-review-a","lane-ows-review-b"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:25:44-04:00"}
{"id":"lane-ows-address","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a5","title":"address findings","done":"2026-09-01T10:25:44-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T10:19:42-04:00","worked":1198,"desc":"The round where the two reviewers' disagreement was SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT rather than by the orchestrator picking a side.\n\nThe author's finding: they were talking about different inputs, and pi refuted a claim grok never made. pi's fact is true — `Cause.hasInterruptsOnly(Cause.empty) === false`, and a stream ending normally never enters the `catchCause` handler at all; the author instrumented it and the handler does not run. But grok's claim was never \"clean ends route through `endOnInterrupt`\". It was that `endOnInterrupt` MANUFACTURES a clean end out of an interrupt-only failure — which is exactly its job — and that the manufactured end lands in the verdict arm.\n\nStaged as the real sequence (live frame → keep-alive death → the re-subscribe's dial landing as the peer closes), it reproduced: `passes taken: 2`, `{settled:false, passed:false, timed_out:false, duration_ms:6}` — the exact nothing-shape this PR exists to kill, re-entering through the arm this PR added. `lastLive` is already set so the loud refusal cannot fire, and no record can answer.\n\nFixed with the discriminator already sitting in the loop: the reader always speaks at least once, so a zero-frame clean end is a probe that got nothing rather than the run answering. Zero frames now fall through to the same re-subscribe a tagged death takes, pinned by a test that fails on the pre-fix code with that verdict.","after":["lane-ows-review-a","lane-ows-review-b"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:25:44-04:00"}
{"id":"lane-ows-ci","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a6","title":"CI green at head","done":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T10:25:53-04:00","worked":1684,"desc":"GREEN on an uncontested darwin re-run, at the human's instruction to re-run cleanly before merging.\n\n**The sequence, with two of the orchestrator's own errors corrected in it.** The first run settled `passed:true` on both platforms via the pool and ci@petit, but GitHub's native `odu (macos-latest, aarch64-darwin)` job failed at the same sha. The orchestrator first diagnosed a commit-status collision — two venues writing one context, a green burying a red — and that was WRONG: the Actions workflow runs with `--no-post`, and every `ci::*` status at that sha was written by the local run alone. It then hypothesised that the macos runner could never substitute or build a freshly-derived `odu-runner`, so the job would be red on every source-changing PR. **The re-run disproves that too: it passed.** The failure was transient.\n\nWhat survives is the smallest of the three claims and the only one that was always true: the watcher launched a run in a repo whose CI launches itself, which the dag forbids. The cost was duplicated darwin work, and it is why a red and a green existed at one sha to be confused in the first place.\n\nFinal state read from GitHub rather than from the watcher: every check passing, both Actions jobs success, `mergeStateStatus` CLEAN, `MERGEABLE`.","after":["lane-ows-merge-master","lane-ows-address"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00","custom":{"kolu-terminal":"8e94ad66","odu-worktree":".worktrees/odu-wait-settle","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/odu/pull/96"}}
{"id":"lane-ows-evidence","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a7","title":"evidence verified","done":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00","desc":"VERIFIED as proof rather than a photograph. The lane's claim was that a wait could report a healthy run as a failed one; the evidence is the reproduction and its reversal, measured against a real cross-process coordinator frozen for 20s — **before: 10.0s → `{settled:false,passed:false}` exit 1; after: 27.6s → `{settled:true,passed:true}` exit 0.**\n\nBeyond that, the MUST the review found was itself reproduced before it was fixed: the staged sequence (live frame → keep-alive death → re-subscribe dial landing as the peer closes) produced `{settled:false,passed:false,timed_out:false,duration_ms:6}` — the exact nothing-shape the PR exists to kill, re-entering through the arm the PR added — and is now pinned by a test that fails on the pre-fix code with that verdict.\n\nAnd the CI watcher dogfooded it: this run's own `wait --settle` was driven through the BRANCH's build rather than master's, single dial, blocked correctly, exited 0 with the right verdict.","after":["lane-ows-ci"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00"}
{"id":"lane-ows-deferrals","parent":"odu-wait-settle-short","ord":"a8","title":"deferrals ruled #human","done":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00","desc":"GATE CLEAR: `## Deferrals` reads `No deferrals.` in every round, and both halves the lane owed — the lying wait and the retry verb with no description — shipped in the one PR, which was the condition set at dispatch.","after":["lane-ows-evidence"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:04:11-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:53:57-04:00"}
